WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
Welcome to week four module one.

2
00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.040
So where you are now is you've done the outreach.

3
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.160
You've got some conversations happening and there's maybe a few interested

4
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:08.600
parties.

5
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.900
So what this module covers is all about turning those conversations into

6
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:13.520
clients,

7
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.280
which by the way, this doesn't require you to be salesy at all.

8
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.560
It's just about understanding their problem and showing that you can solve it.

9
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.520
So it's all about formulating solutions for people.

10
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.720
And the process to get there is quite simple really.

11
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.120
There's this framework that we use internally called dots, the ODS.

12
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.040
And this is just a nice and simple way for you to remember that for each client

13
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:33.520
that you serve,

14
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.640
this would be the general process that you can follow.

15
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.320
So these stands for discovery, which is learning about the situation of your

16
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:39.680
client,

17
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.200
their problem and what they need.

18
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.800
Then once you understand the needs of your client, you then present them with

19
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:44.360
your offer,

20
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.560
your proposal, which is what the O stands for.

21
00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.480
So this is what you'll do exactly.

22
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.140
What are the deliverables, how long it takes and how much you would charge for

23
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:52.280
it.

24
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:56.310
T is all about tailoring that offer based on their feedback and we'll go into

25
00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:56.960
this deeper.

26
00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.280
But generally, when you first present the offer, the client may have some

27
00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:01.040
feedback on it.

28
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.440
And so this phase is all about tailoring this proposal to whatever they need in

29
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:06.280
order to arrive

30
00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.800
at a mutually beneficial contract.

31
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.000
And then finally, S is all about seal.

32
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.680
So this is the process of getting that final yes, locking it in, having the

33
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:14.360
contract side,

34
00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.800
which is very important and also collecting the payment.

35
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:17.160
That's it.

36
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.880
Very simple discovery offer, tailor and seal.

37
00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.200
So let's go to each one.

38
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.880
The first one is discovery.

39
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.110
So this is pretty standard for each and every engagement that you will

40
00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:27.800
encounter,

41
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.200
where when you get connected to a client or a potential client, you set up a

42
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:31.960
discovery call,

43
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.280
which can be as short as 30 minutes, sometimes even 15 minutes is fine.

44
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:40.600
If you're dealing with larger enterprise clients, it's also an option to do an

45
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:41.440
hour long call.

46
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.780
But that would be probably reserved for bigger clients that are, let's say, big

47
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:46.640
corporations.

48
00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.440
The purpose of this call is very simple.

49
00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:52.110
Just think of discovery as literally that discovering the situation of your

50
00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:52.480
client

51
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.280
so that you can see where you can help in.

52
00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.600
And so what's important to remember here is that your role for this specific

53
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.000
meeting

54
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.000
is to ask questions and listen, not to pitch yet.

55
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.490
And just to help you out, there's a meeting document template that you can

56
00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:04.200
check out below.

57
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.080
And that would just let you capture notes throughout that meeting as well.

58
00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.000
And of course, you can try out some of the note taking AI tools like FADOM or

59
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:11.560
Firefly

60
00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.160
to help you with this mean at taking.

61
00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.360
It also lends itself to your credential being an AI practitioner.

62
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.360
If you start using AI tools in the very first discovery call itself.

63
00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:23.120
Now, if you're talking to a larger team or an enterprise client,

64
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.160
it's actually better to have a simple slide back.

65
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.970
One or two slides that basically introduces who you are before going into the

66
00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:29.560
discussion.

67
00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.640
And that slide back, you should have already prepared something coming from

68
00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.560
your previous week's modules.

69
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.120
Now what it is called recall, what I find is confidence really is key.

70
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.640
And you just need to keep it simple.

71
00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.400
If you're operating at the same level as the person that you are going to pitch

72
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:43.600
to,

73
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.240
then the conversation will flow better.

74
00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.480
So I think as a general principle, it's always good to just keep it simple.

75
00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.800
And so the framework that I recommend you to use is a very simple 5 WH

76
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.080
framework.

77
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.280
So since your role here is to ask questions,

78
00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.400
just always think to write down something around what, who, where, when, why,

79
00:02:59.400 --> 00:02:59.920
and how.

80
00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.240
So what is their goal?

81
00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:02.680
What is it that they're trying to achieve?

82
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:03.680
Is it more customers?

83
00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.080
Is it trying to find more leads?

84
00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.920
Is it a specific operational problem that they need to automate?

85
00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.120
And then you need to ask them who they want to impact,

86
00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.560
especially if the task is around getting more customers or serving customers,

87
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.230
then you would need to understand who their ICP is or their ideal customer

88
00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:17.720
profile.

89
00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.280
You also need to understand where we are now in this specific goal

90
00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.120
and when they want this to be achieved.

91
00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.120
And finally, why are we doing this?

92
00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.320
And if there's already any ideas on how this can be done.

93
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.520
So I think this is a good general purpose framework that you can apply

94
00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:33.880
no matter what discovery piece that you are doing.

95
00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:37.520
So it's whether building agents for creative work or building AI agents

96
00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.720
to optimize or automate operations, boiling it down to these simple questions

97
00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.820
is going to get you far to understand how to specifically serve that client of

98
00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:46.200
yours.

99
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.240
And like I mentioned, just let them talk take notes,

100
00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.720
understand their problem deeply and just take the time to build that

101
00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:51.680
relationship as well,

102
00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.340
especially as we get to the further modules when it's time to expand the

103
00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:56.160
further client basis.

104
00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:57.920
And as you start to serve more clients,

105
00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.640
you'll understand that relationship really is key across this whole thing.

106
00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.640
Sometimes it's not even your technical competence or the latest AI models.

107
00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.480
You know, when you are talking to clients who are all business oriented

108
00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.680
and couldn't care less what AI model you use,

109
00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:15.000
it's always the client relationship that will get you far in this field.

110
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.640
And then very simply after that 15 to 30 minute conversation is over,

111
00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.280
you just close the call with a quick summary.

112
00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.520
So we just say that you'll put something together offline

113
00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:26.200
and that you can go from there once you send out that offer or that proposal.

114
00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.400
So that covers discovery.

115
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:31.480
Often a quick 15 minute call can already give you a lot of information

116
00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:34.240
around what offer you want to provide to the client,

117
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:35.920
which is the next phase of this framework.

118
00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.160
Now, the offer is usually sent asynchronously,

119
00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.320
meaning after the discovery call, when you go your separate ways,

120
00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.480
you draft your proposal, which is typically a slide pack or a document,

121
00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:49.400
or even sometimes a cleanly laid out PDF that you send to your client via email

122
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:49.400
.

123
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:50.760
And the structure of it that I recommend,

124
00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.480
there's a sample that you can check out below, which will go to in a bit.

125
00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:57.040
But from our practice, it's always good to just have like one to two slides

126
00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.640
in there introducing your firm.

127
00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.200
Because remember, when these slide packs are being sent,

128
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.410
it's very possible that your prospective client will forward it within their

129
00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:04.600
team

130
00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:06.840
who have not met you yet, or even in the future,

131
00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.320
forward it to other potential clients when they refer you out.

132
00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.530
And so it's always good to have that one to two slide introduction around what

133
00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:14.360
it is that you do.

134
00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.480
And to give a quick and simple example,

135
00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:20.480
you can see we have this Robolabs company snapshot in a client prospect that we

136
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:21.160
sent before.

137
00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.800
And we sent this as a PDF where in the very first slide,

138
00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.480
we just have like a written note around who we are,

139
00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.180
so that they understand what it is that we do and the technologies that we work

140
00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:30.560
against.

141
00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.230
And because at least during this time, the concept of AI agents are still

142
00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:34.920
pretty new.

143
00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.400
And it still is for a lot of people at the time of this recording.

144
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:41.360
We also included a slide six-plaining what AI agents are in the first place,

145
00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.680
just so that the other party can understand what it is that we are actually

146
00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:45.360
offering them.

147
00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:50.320
And then we also included even a slide around the common benefits on AI agents,

148
00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:52.360
which this slide you can just get it below,

149
00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.680
and you can just translate it to your specific design and your specific

150
00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.040
aesthetic for your service.

151
00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.880
But it's always good to just translate all of the technical jargon that we're

152
00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:00.320
learning

153
00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:02.600
into key benefits that everyone understands.

154
00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.600
Because at the end of the day, at least in our view in Robolabs,

155
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.840
AI agents, the core benefit of them is that they let you save time,

156
00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.440
they let you reach more people to your customers,

157
00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.640
and they also let you create more output.

158
00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:17.100
So better products, better output, whatever it is that they are doing, you can

159
00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:17.760
do more.

160
00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.360
And here at the bottom, if in case they need more information,

161
00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:23.480
we included some example clients there as well.

162
00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:24.560
And so that's the first part.

163
00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.720
And once you have that, that's pretty standard across any proposal that you

164
00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:27.920
send.

165
00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.080
So that number one, you officialize the kind of practice that you have.

166
00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:34.080
And number two, whenever this PDF gets shared around,

167
00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.000
you are properly introduced every time.

168
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.520
But the meat of this offer is obviously the offer itself, right?

169
00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:42.200
And so what you would need is some sort of visual or slide

170
00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:45.800
with information framing the problem based on what you learned in discovery,

171
00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:50.040
just to do a recap and also a slide on your process, so how you will deliver.

172
00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.250
And on this topic, there's actually two options or two ways to package your

173
00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:53.640
offer.

174
00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:57.240
The first one is best for systems-based automations.

175
00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:00.840
And while the second one is more for a deliverable-based engagement.

176
00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.680
And just to differentiate between the two in case you don't know the variation

177
00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.560
between them is actually very simple.

178
00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:08.880
So if you're working with a client and what you're selling is the system

179
00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.960
to, let's say, automate their operations,

180
00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.640
then you're in a client engagement that is system-based,

181
00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:16.360
where your key question will most likely be something along the lines of,

182
00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.640
how do I make this AI agent workflow part of their process?

183
00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:23.520
Now, this is pretty common when it comes to automating operations, for example.

184
00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.200
But usually the challenge here is how do you actually integrate

185
00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.360
whatever automation or agent that you build into their business?

186
00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.080
Because by the end of this engagement, you hand over whatever system

187
00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:36.240
that you create or whatever agent that you create into the hands of the client,

188
00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:36.440
right?

189
00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:39.680
And that the remaining thing that you do is just tuning and further support.

190
00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:42.560
Contrast this with a deliverable-based engagement,

191
00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:44.600
which is pretty common in the creative field.

192
00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:48.560
Like if you're delivering, let's say, UGCs for your client or even clips

193
00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:50.000
from their long-form videos.

194
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.600
The key difference here is what you are selling is the output itself.

195
00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:55.400
So a common question that you will ask is,

196
00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:59.440
"Hey, you have this AI agent on your side and you operate the AI agent."

197
00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:02.880
But whatever output that it generates, let's say the content or the clips

198
00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:07.200
or the UGC AI content, that is what you sell to the client on a deliverable-

199
00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:08.040
based charging.

200
00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:10.120
And so client integration isn't really needed

201
00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:12.400
because the creatives or the output is what you sell.

202
00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:15.720
Now, this is an important distinction because if you're in a systems-based

203
00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:16.240
engagement,

204
00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:18.640
the way that you structure offer will be entirely different

205
00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.560
versus if you are working on a deliverable-based engagement.

206
00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:25.020
So to give an example, when we make, say, new systems or new automations for

207
00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:25.360
clients,

208
00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.460
the framework that we use is this BIT framework where you build, integrate, and

209
00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:29.800
tune.

210
00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:32.680
And I'll just give you a quick example from an actual proposal.

211
00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:33.920
So that you can understand it better.

212
00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.240
So this is a standard slide that we have around introducing the BIT framework

213
00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.480
for developing AI agents that we just provide to our clients

214
00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:45.320
so that they can understand how we work as well in developing these systems.

215
00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:48.880
You can just grab this slide, again, customize it to whatever color themes

216
00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.320
that you want for your branding and for your practice.

217
00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:55.080
But what this basically covers is those three phases that we talked about.

218
00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:56.880
First, you have the build phase.

219
00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.120
And this is where you create the AI agent.

220
00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:01.200
In a staging environment is what we call it.

221
00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:03.080
And mostly, this is your system.

222
00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:06.740
So if you're working on your laptop and it's just you, for example, at the

223
00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:06.920
moment,

224
00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:09.120
then the staging environment is your own device.

225
00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:12.920
But what you will do is build it in such a way that you can end in milestone

226
00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:13.280
one

227
00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.480
where you can demo this to your client.

228
00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:18.690
And it's important to communicate this so that you can also clarify the

229
00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:19.600
expectations

230
00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:21.280
from the client side, right?

231
00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:24.940
Because they need to understand that this phase one where you're building out a

232
00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:25.520
demo for them

233
00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:29.750
already involves work and that has a bit of translation on how you charge for

234
00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:30.200
the client,

235
00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:32.000
which I'll go into in the pricing section.

236
00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.030
But once you demo it to the client and they are happy with it, then we go to

237
00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:35.440
phase two,

238
00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:38.880
which is integrating that system that you built onto their side.

239
00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:42.520
So you connect the AI agent to their systems and to their business operations.

240
00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:46.440
So this process is where you assist them with integrating with their systems

241
00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:46.840
and ops.

242
00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:50.960
So let's say they're using Notion for setting up their content calendar,

243
00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:54.480
or they're using Postgres SQL for their database.

244
00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.480
Then you think about that integration.

245
00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:59.200
And we'll discuss this more in the delivery part of this course.

246
00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:03.690
But for most cases, you get some sort of a login or credential in order to put

247
00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:04.480
your system

248
00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:08.480
or your AI agent into whatever software it is that they are using at the moment

249
00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:08.480
.

250
00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:11.080
